    <!-- ===== KEY TERMS ===== -->
    <h2>🔑 Key Terms</h2>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Compression</strong></td><td>The process of reducing the size of data to save storage space or transmission bandwidth.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Lossless compression</strong></td><td>A compression method where all original data is retained, allowing the exact reconstruction of the original file without any loss of quality.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Lossy compression</strong></td><td>A compression method where some original data is permanently removed, leading to a smaller file size but a loss of quality.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Run-length encoding (RLE)</strong></td><td>A lossless compression technique that replaces sequences of identical data values with a count and the single value.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Transform coding</strong></td><td>A lossy compression technique that converts data into a different domain (e.g., frequency domain) to remove less important information.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Redundancy</strong></td><td>Data that is repeated or unnecessary; compression works by removing this.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <!-- ===== COMPARISON TABLES ===== -->
    <h2>📊 Lossless vs. Lossy Compression</h2>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Lossless Compression</th><th>Lossy Compression</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Reversible?</strong></td><td>Yes – exact original data can be reconstructed.</td><td>No – some data is permanently lost.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Compression Ratio</strong></td><td>Lower (less reduction in size).</td><td>Higher (greater reduction in size).</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Data Integrity</strong></td><td>Perfect – no quality loss.</td><td>Approximate – quality is degraded.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Typical Use</strong></td><td>Text files, executable programs, databases, archives.</td><td>Images, audio, video where imperceptible loss is acceptable.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Common Methods</strong></td><td>Run-Length Encoding (RLE), Huffman Coding, Lempel-Ziv (LZ).</td><td>Transform Coding (DCT in JPEG), Quantization, Psychoacoustic models.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Example Formats</strong></td><td>PNG, ZIP, FLAC, GIF.</td><td>JPEG, MP3, MPEG, H.264.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>📊 Compression Algorithms: RLE vs. Transform Coding</h2>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>Type</th><th>How it works</th><th>Best for</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Run-Length Encoding (RLE)</strong></td>
                <td>Lossless</td>
                <td>Replaces consecutive repeated values (runs) with a single value and a count.</td>
                <td>Data with long repeated sequences: simple images, icons, text with repeated characters.</td>
                <td>Original: <code>AAAAABBBCCDAA</code><br>Compressed: <code>5A3B2C1D2A</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Transform Coding</strong></td>
                <td>Lossy</td>
                <td>Transforms data from time/spatial domain to frequency domain (e.g., DCT), quantizes coefficients to remove fine details, then encodes.</td>
                <td>Images (JPEG), audio (MP3), video (MPEG).</td>
                <td>JPEG divides image into 8×8 blocks, applies DCT, quantizes high-frequency coefficients to zero.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

